current_protocol/sources/web_search_records.json:4:  "result": "SI 2026 Forecasting & Empirical Methods | NBER (https://www.nber.org/conferences/si-2026-forecasting-empirical-methods)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; FOMC In Silico: A Multi-Agent System for Monetary Policy Decision Modeling ... In a backtest of 218 FOMC meetings from 2000 through June 2026, the framework predicts the policy rate with a mean absolute error of 8.3 basis points, comes within 25 basis points in 94 percent of meetings, and correctly classifies hikes, cuts, and holds in 91 percent.\n\nRaimondo Pala, Tor Vergata University of Rome\n\nView abstract\n\nThis paper discusses identification, estimation, and inference on dynamic local average treatment effects (LATEs) in instrumental variables (IVs) settings. First, we show that compliers—observations whose treatment status is affected by the instrument—can be identified individually in time series data using smoothness assumptions and local comparisons of treatment assignments. Second, we show that this result enables not only better interpretability of IV estimates but also direct testing of the exclusion restriction by comparing outcomes among identified non-compliers across instrument values. Third, we document pervasive weak identification in applied work using IVs with time series data by surveying recent publications in leading economics journals. However, we find that strong identification often holds in large subsamples for which the instrument induces changes in the treatment. Motivated by this, we introduce a method based on dynamic programming to detect the most strongly-identified subsample and show how to use this subsample to improve estimation and inference. We also develop new identification-robust inference procedures that focus on the most strongly-identified subsample, offering efficiency gains relative to existing full sample identification-robust inference when identification fails over parts of the sample. Finally, we apply our results to heteroskedasticity-based identification of monetary policy effects. We find that about 60% of observations are compliers (i.e., cases where the variance of the policy shifts up on FOMC announcement days), and we fail to reject the exclusion restriction. Estimation using the most strongly-identified subsample helps reconcile conflicting IV and GMM estimates in the literature.\n\n12:00 pm\n\nAdjourn and Lunch\n\nA structural model replicating these shifts reveals that the rise of IPP at the expense of Durable Goods is largely explained by increases in the efficiency of IPP inputs used in production: input-biased technical change. Trend variations in sectoral total factor productivity, and their attendant effects on relative prices and income, are the main driver of evolving consumption patterns. Both reduced-form and structural forecasts project these trends to continue over the next two decades, albeit at lower rates, indicating a slower pace of structural change.\n\nThis paper was distributed as Working Paper 34338, where an updated version may be available.\n\nSlides\n\n9:15 am\n\nFOMC In Silico: A Multi-Agent System for Monetary Policy Decision Modeling\n\nSophia Kazinnik, Stanford University\n\nTara M. Sinclair, The George Washington University\n\nView abstract\n\nWe build a multi-agent LLM framework that simulates the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) decision process. Each agent in the simulation represents an FOMC member and receives real-time macroeconomic data, district level conditions, and other relevant information. In a backtest of 218 FOMC meetings from 2000 through June 2026, the framework predicts the policy rate with a mean absolute error of 8.3 basis points, comes within 25 basis points in 94 percent of meetings, and correctly classifies hikes, cuts, and holds in 91 percent. Accuracy is similar before and after the model’s training cutoff, suggesting the results are not driven by memorization. We then use the validated framework for counterfactual experiments. Committee rules can block a politically captured chair, but not public persuasion; committee decisions differ from those produced by mechanical decision rules; composition matters far more than the chair or formal mandate; and deliberation builds consensus rather than improving forecasts. The framework offers a new laboratory for studying monetary policymaking and institutional design.\n\n10:00 am\n\nBreak\n\n10:30 am\n\nChronos-2: From Univariate to Universal Forecasting\n\nPablo A. Guerron, Boston College\n\n11:15 am\n\nThe Prestakes of Stock Market Investing\n\nFrancesco Bianchi, Johns Hopkins University and NBER\n\nDo Q. Lee, New York University\n\nSydney C. Ludvigson, New York University and NBER\n\nSai Ma, Federal Reserve Board of Governors\n\nView abstract\n\nThe Prestakes of Stock Market Investing\n\n12:00 pm\n\nAdjourn and Lunch--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://conference.nber.org/agenda/simple_printable?conf_id=SI26EFFE)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Image  | SI 2026 Forecasting & Empirical Methods ... 9:15 am  | FOMC In Silico: A Multi-Agent System for Monetary Policy Decision Modeling Sophia Kazinnik, Stanford University\nOtto Kuusela, Forecasting Research Institute\nJason Abaluck, Yale University and NBER\nKevin A. Bryan, University of Toronto and NBER\nBasil Halperin, University of Virginia\nTodd R. Jones, Mississippi State University\nConnacher Murphy, Stanford University\nPhilip Trammell, Stanford University\nMatt Reynolds, Forecasting Research Institute\nDan Mayland, Forecasting Research Institute\n12:00 pm  | Adjourn and Lunch\n | Friday, July 17\n8:00 am  | Coffee and Pastries\n8:30 am  | The Past and Future of U.S. Structural Change: Compositional Accounting and Forecasting (slides) Andrew Foerster, Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco\nAndreas Hornstein, Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond\nPierre-Daniel Sarte, Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond\nMark W. Watson, Princeton University and NBER\n9:15 am  | FOMC In Silico: A Multi-Agent System for Monetary Policy Decision Modeling Sophia Kazinnik, Stanford University\nTara M. Sinclair, The George Washington University\n10:00 am  | Break\n10:30 am  | Chronos-2: From Univariate to Universal Forecasting Pablo A. Guerron, Boston College\n11:15 am  | The Prestakes of Stock Market Investing Francesco Bianchi, Johns Hopkins University and NBER\nDo Q. Lee, New York University\nSydney C. Ludvigson, New York University and NBER\nSai Ma, Federal Reserve Board of Governors\n12:00 pm  | Adjourn and Lunch\n\nParticipants--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNBER Summer Institute 2026 — Video Library (https://gideon-bornstein.com/si2026.html)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Sinclair ... Sophia Kazinnik, Tara M. ... In a backtest of 218 FOMC meetings from 2000 through June 2026, the framework predicts the policy rate with a mean absolute error of 8.3 basis points, comes within 25 basis points in 94 percent of meetings, and correctly classifies hikes, cuts, and holds in 91 percent. ... Watch 6:08:01 Paper PDF\nSophia Kazinnik, Tara M. Sinclair\n\nWatch 51:12Paper PDF[Button: + Abstract]\n\nWe build a multi-agent LLM framework that simulates the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) decision process. Each agent in the simulation represents an FOMC member and receives real-time macroeconomic data, district level conditions, and other relevant information. In a backtest of 218 FOMC meetings from 2000 through June 2026, the framework predicts the policy rate with a mean absolute error of 8.3 basis points, comes within 25 basis points in 94 percent of meetings, and correctly classifies hikes, cuts, and holds in 91 percent. Accuracy is similar before and after the model’s training cutoff, suggesting the results are not driven by memorization. We then use the validated framework for c\n\n10:30 am\n\nChronos-2: From Univariate to Universal Forecasting\n\nPablo A. Guerron\n\nWatch 2:05:04Paper PDF\n\n11:15 am\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFOMC <i>In Silico</i>: A Multi-Agent System for Monetary Policy Decision Modeling by Sophia Kazinnik, Tara M. Sinclair :: SSRN (https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=5424097)\nciteturn3search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; In a backtest of 218 FOMC meetings from 2000 through June 2026, the framework predicts the policy rate with a mean absolute error of 8.3 basis points, comes within 25 basis points in 94 percent of meetings, and correctly classifies hikes, cuts, and holds in 91 percent. ... Kazinnik, Sophia and Sinclair, Tara M., FOMC In Silico: A Multi-Agent System for Monetary Policy Decision Modeling (August 31, 2025). ... Open PDF in Browser\n\n# FOMC In Silico: A Multi-Agent System for Monetary Policy Decision Modeling\n\n52 Pages Posted: 8 Sep 2025 Last revised: 12 Jul 2026\n\nSee all articles by Sophia Kazinnik\n\n## Sophia Kazinnik\n\nStanford University\n\n## Tara M. Sinclair\n\nGeorge Washington University - Department of Economics; George Washington University - Elliott School of International Affairs (ESIA); George Washington University - Institute For International Economic Policy (GWIIEP); George Washington University - Research Program on Forecasting; George Washington University - George Washington Institute of Public Policy (GWIPP); Australian National University (ANU) - Centre for Applied Macroeconomic Analysis (CAMA); Halle Institute for Economic Research\n\nDate Written: August 31, 2025\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe build a multi-agent LLM framework that simulates the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) decision process. Each agent in the simulation represents an FOMC member and receives real-time macroeconomic data, district level conditions, and other relevant information. In a backtest of 218 FOMC meetings from 2000 through June 2026, the framework predicts the policy rate with a mean absolute error of 8.3 basis points, comes within 25 basis points in 94 percent of meetings, and correctly classifies hikes, cuts, and holds in 91 percent. Accuracy is similar before and after the model’s training cutoff, suggesting the results are not driven by memorization. We then use the validated framework for counterfactual experiments. Committee rules can block a politically captured chair, but not public persuasion; committee decisions differ  from those produced by mechanical decision rules; composition matters far more than the chair or formal mandate; and deliberation builds consensus rather than improving forecasts. The framework offers a new laboratory for studying monetary policymaking and institutional design.\n\nKeywords: Generative AI, Multi-Agent Systems, Large Language Models, Federal Open Market Committee, Monetary Policy, Simulations\n\nLicense Information\n\nThe copyright holder has granted SSRN a license. All rights reserved. No reuse allowed without permission.\n\nJEL Classification: E52, E58, C63, D83, C73\n\nSuggested Citation\n\nKazinnik, Sophia and Sinclair, Tara M., FOMC In Silico: A Multi-Agent System for Monetary Policy Decision Modeling (August 31, 2025). Available at SSRN: https://ssrn.com/abstract=5424097 or http://dx.doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.5424097\n\n### Sophia Kazinnik (Contact Author)\n\n#### Stanford University ( email )\n\n367 Panama St\nStanford, CA 94305\nUnited States\n\n### Tara M. Sinclair\n\n#### George Washington University - Department of Economics ( email )\n\n2115 G Street NW\nMonroe Hall Suite 340\nWashington, DC 20052\nUnited States\n202-944-7988 (Phone)\n202-994-6147 (Fax)\n\n\nDownload This Paper\n\nOpen PDF in Browser\n\n### 43 References\n\n## Paper statistics\n\nDownloads\n\n987\n\nAbstract Views\n\n2,885\n\n43 References--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nApril 20, 2026 (https://www.sophiakazinnik.com/cv.pdf)\nciteturn3search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; • “Let’s Face It: Quantifying the Impact of Nonverbal Communication in FOMC Press Conferences”. ... International Review of Economics and Finance, 76 (2021) ... • “AI and the Fed” (with Erik Brynjolfsson; NBER Working Paper). ... • “FOMC In Silico: A Multi-Agent System for Monetary Policy Modeling” (with Tara Sinclair). ... Quantitative Analysis).\nPublications (Peer-Reviewed)\n• “Does Media Sentiment Influence Bank Supervision?” Accepted at Journal of Financial Stability (with David\nAldama-Navarrette, Filippo Curti, and Anne Hansen).\n• “News and Networks: Using Financial News Coverage to Measure Bank Interconnectedness”. Accepted at Journal of\nBanking & Finance (with Daniela Scida, Cooper Killen, and John Wu).\n• “Bank Run, Interrupted: Modeling Deposit Withdrawals with Generative AI”. Management Science, forthcoming, 2026.\n• “Let’s Face It: Quantifying the Impact of Nonverbal Communication in FOMC Press Conferences”. Journal of\nMonetary Economics, 139 (Oct 2023) (with Filippo Curti).\n• “User Experience, Visual Aesthetics, and Content of Central Bank Websites”. Journal of Economic Behavior &\nOrganization (with Filippo Curti).\n• “Cyber Risk Definition and Classification for Financial Risk Management”. Journal of Operational Risk, 2023 (with\nFilippo Curti, Jeffrey Gerlach, Michael Lee, Atanas Mihov).\n• “Text Mining Methodologies with R: An Application to Central Bank Texts”. David Nguyen, and Jiaxin Pei).\n• “What do LLMs Want?” (with Thomas R. Cook, Zach Modig, and Nathan M. Palmer).\n• “AISPA: Artificial Intelligence System Prompt Assurance” (with Xiangning Lin, Jiaxin Pei, Erik Brynjolfsson, Alex\nPentland, and others).\n• “SurplusGPT: Surplus Measurement at Scale” (with Erik Brynjolfsson).\n• “Ex Machina: Financial Stability in the Age of Artificial Intelligence” (with Kartik Anand, Agnese Leonello, and Ettore\nPanetti).\n• “Social Group Bias in AI Finance” (with Thomas R. Cook).\n• “Simulating the Survey of Professional Forecasters” (with Anne L. Hansen, John J. Horton, Daniela Puzzello, and Ali\nZarifhonar).\n• “FOMC In Silico: A Multi-Agent System for Monetary Policy Modeling” (with Tara Sinclair).\n• “Evaluating Local Language Models: An Application to Financial Earnings Calls” (with Anne Hansen, Thomas R.\nCook, and Peter McAdam).\n• “Can ChatGPT Decipher Fedspeak?” (with Anne L. Hansen; Reject & Resubmit at Journal of Financial and\nQuantitative Analysis).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSophia Kazinnik - Research Scientist (https://www.sophiakazinnik.com/)\nciteturn3search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; I study how people, institutions, and AI systems make decisions, and how those decisions shape economic outcomes. ... My current research includes simulations of Federal Reserve policy meetings, experiments on how AI agents behave in financial markets, and new ways to measure how people use and value AI. ...   * Jul 17·Presented FOMC In Silico at the NBER Summer Institute ...   * May 8·Presenting FOMC In Silico at the 2nd Workshop on AI Behavioral Science, Stanford SIEPR\n# Sophia\nKazinnik\n\nResearch Scientist, Digital Economy Lab, Stanford HAI\n\nHow do people, institutions, and AI agents make decisions?\n\nAnd how can we measure the decision processes that are not directly observable?\n\nkazinnik@stanford.edu\n\nI study how people, institutions, and AI systems make decisions, and how those decisions shape economic outcomes. My work combines economics, behavioral methods, and artificial intelligence to examine what happens when decisions are made under uncertainty, when people respond to machines, and when multiple AI agents interact with one another.\n\nMy current research includes simulations of Federal Reserve policy meetings, experiments on how AI agents behave in financial markets, and new ways to measure how people use and value AI. Across these projects, I use language models not only as tools, but also as objects of economic study.\n\nBefore joining Stanford, I spent seven years as a financial economist and quant at the Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond, where I worked on bank supervision and financial risk. That experience continues to shape how I think about institutions, incentives, and decision making.\n\n### Research Interests\n\nAI Agents, AI & Economic Measurement, Behavioral Finance, Policy Communication\n\nDownload CV\n\n### Recent & Upcoming\n\n  * Jul 17·Presented FOMC In Silico at the NBER Summer Institute\n  * Jul 10·\"Financial Stability in the Age of AI\" at the EDSS conference, Banque de France\n  * Jun 27·Keynote at the South African Reserve Bank: \"The New Financial Stability Problem\"\n  * Jun 2·Bank of Korea: talk on the use of AI by central banks\n  * May 19·Invited seminar at the National Bank of Belgium\n  * May 8·Presenting FOMC In Silico at the 2nd Workshop on AI Behavioral Science, Stanford SIEPR\n  * Apr 24·NYU Stern 7th Annual Volatility and Risk Institute Conference: \"AI and Risk Management\"\n  * Apr 13–14·Presented SurplusGPT at AI and the Economy Inaugural Workshop, Penn State"
research/source_ledgers/literature/current_oct09/web_retrieval_records.json:3:  "opened": "Internal Error ()\nciteturn120view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=5424097\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=5424097: (403) Forbidden\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFOMC In Silico: A Multi-Agent System for Monetary Policy Decision Modeling - Stanford Digital Economy Lab (https://digitaleconomy.stanford.edu/publication/fomc-in-silico-a-multi-agent-system-for-monetary-policy-decision-modeling/)\nciteturn120view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://digitaleconomy.stanford.edu/publication/fomc-in-silico-a-multi-agent-system-for-monetary-policy-decision-modeling/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 73\nL0:   * cite0†Stanford University†www.stanford.edu L1:   * cite1†The Stanford Institute for Human-Centered AI†hai.stanford.edu L2: \nL3:   * [Button: Research ]\nL4:     * cite2†Economics of Transformative AI Revolutionizing economics to harness the full potential of advanced AI. L5:     * cite3†New Measures of the Economy Inventing tools and metrics for an AI-driven economy that supports shared prosperity. L6:     * cite4†Digital Platforms and Society Building foundations for\nL7: pro-social human behavior and AI-empowered organizations. L8:     * cite5†AI Agents Developing agentic AI frameworks grounded in consumer-centric values for social science research and to advance human interests. L9:     * cite6†Research overview → L10:     * cite7†Explore all work → L11: Close\nL12: \nL13: [Input: Search...] Search\nL14: \nL15: Featured searches\nL16: \nL17:   * cite8†machine learning how did we get here L18:   * cite9†canaries in the coal mine L19:   * cite10†transformative AI L20:   * cite11†Digitalist Papers L21: \nL22:   1. cite6†← Research L23:   2. cite7†← All Work L24: # FOMC In Silico: A Multi-Agent System for Monetary Policy Decision Modeling\nL25: \nL26:   * Working Paper\nL27: \nL28: Date\nL29: \nL30: 09/02/2025\nL31: \nL32: Read this publication\nL33: \nL34: cite12†SSRN†papers.ssrn.com L35: \nL36: Authors\nL37: \nL38: cite13†Sophia Kazinnik , Tara M. Sinclair\nL39: \nL40: Share this publication\nL41: \nL42: cite12†copy link†papers.ssrn.com L43: We develop a multi-agent framework for modeling the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) decision making process. The framework combines two approaches: an LLM-based simulation and a Monte Carlo implementation of a generalized Bayesian voting model. Both begin from identical prior beliefs about the appropriate interest rate for each committee member, formed using real-time data and member profiles.\nL44: In a simulation replicating the July 2025 FOMC meeting, both tracks deliver rates near the 4.25-4.50% range’s upper end (4.42% LLM, 4.38% MC). Political pressure scenario increases dissent and dispersion: the LLM track averages 4.38% and shows dissent in 88% of meetings; the MC track averages 4.39% and shows dissent in 61% of meetings.\nL45: A negative jobs revision scenario moves outcomes lower: LLM at 4.30% (dissent in 74% of meeting), and MC at 4.32% (dissent in 62% of meeting), with final decisions remaining inside the 4.25-4.50% range. The framework isolates small, scenario-dependent wedges between behavioral and rational baselines, offering an in silico environment for counterfactual evaluation in monetary policy.\nL46: 353 Jane Stanford Way\nL47: Stanford University\nL48: Stanford, CA 94305\nL49: \nL50: digitaleconomylab@stanford.edu\nL51: ## Get Involved\nL52: \nL53:   * cite14†Courses & Education L54:   * cite15†Opportunities L55:   * cite16†Support L56:   * cite17†Corporate Affiliates L57: \nL58: cite18†Image L59: \nL60:   * cite0†Stanford Home†www.stanford.edu L61:   * cite1†Stanford HAI†hai.stanford.edu L62:   * cite19†Maps & Directions†visit.stanford.edu L63:   * cite20†Search Stanford†www.stanford.edu L64:   * cite21†Emergency Info†emergency.stanford.edu L65:   * cite22†Terms of Use†www.stanford.edu L66:   * cite23†Privacy†www.stanford.edu L67:   * cite24†Copyright†uit.stanford.edu L68:   * cite25†Trademarks†adminguide.stanford.edu L69:   * cite26†Non-Discrimination†exploredegrees.stanford.edu L70:   * cite27†Accessibility†www.stanford.edu L71: \nL72: © Stanford University. Stanford, California 94305.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www2.gwu.edu/~forcpgm/2025-005.pdf)\nciteturn120view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 52; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www2.gwu.edu/~forcpgm/2025-005.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1726\nL0@P0: Working Papers represent preliminary work circulated for comment and discussion. Please contact the\nL1@P0: author(s) before citing this paper in any publications. The views expressed in Working Papers are solely\nL2@P0: those of the author(s) and do not necessarily represent the views of the H. O. Stekler Research Program on\nL3@P0: Forecasting, the Department of Economics, the Columbian College, or the George Washington University.\nL4@P0: WORKING PAPER SERIES\nL5@P0: FOMC In Silico: A Multi-Agent System for\nL6@P0: Monetary Policy Decision Modeling\nL7@P0: Sophia Kazinnik\nL8@P0: Tara M. Sinclair\nL9@P0: Working Paper No. 2025-005\nL10@P0: August, 2025\nL11@P0: H. O. STEKLER RESEARCH PROGRAM ON FORECASTING\nL12@P0: Department of Economics\nL13@P0: Columbian College of Arts & Sciences\nL14@P0: The George Washington University\nL15@P0: Washington, DC 20052\nL16@P0-1: https://cer.columbian.gwu.edu/ho-stekler-research-program-forecastingFOMC In Silico: A Multi-Agent System\nL17@P1: for Monetary Policy Decision Modeling\nL18@P1: This is a preliminary draft. The authors welcome all comments and suggestions.\nL19@P1: Sophia Kazinnik∗ Tara M. Sinclair†\nL20@P1: August 31, 2025\nL21@P1: Abstract\nL22@P1: We develop a multi-agent framework for modeling the Federal Open Market\nL23@P1: Committee (FOMC) decision making process. The framework combines two\nL24@P1: approaches: an LLM-based simulation and a Monte Carlo implementation of\nL25@P1: a generalized Bayesian voting model. Both begin from identical prior beliefs\nL26@P1: about the appropriate interest rate for each committee member, formed using\nL27@P1: real-time data and member profiles. In a simulation replicating the July 2025\nL28@P1: FOMC meeting, both tracks deliver rates near the 4.25–4.50% range’s upper\nL29@P1: end (4.42% LLM, 4.38% MC). Political pressure scenario increases dissent\nL30@P1: and dispersion: the LLM track averages 4.38% and shows dissent in 88% of\nL31@P1: meetings; the MC track averages 4.39% and shows dissent in 61% of meetings.\nL32@P1: A negative jobs revision scenario moves outcomes lower: LLM at 4.30% (dissent\nL33@P1: in 74% of meeting), and MC at 4.32% (dissent in 62% of meeting), with final\nL34@P1: decisions remaining inside the 4.25-4.50% range. The framework isolates small,\nL35@P1: scenario-dependent wedges between behavioral and rational baselines, offering\nL36@P1: an in silico environment for counterfactual evaluation in monetary policy.\nL37@P1: Keywords: Generative AI; Multi-Agent Systems; Large Language Models,\nL38@P1: Federal Open Market Committee; Monetary Policy; Simulations\nL39@P1: JEL Codes: E52, E58, C63, D83, C73\nL40@P1: ∗Email: kazinnik@stanford.edu.\nL41@P1: †Email: tsinc@gwu.edu.\nL42@P1: We thank Erik Brynjolfsson, Thomas R. Cook, Seung Jung Lee, Andrew Martinez, and Daniela\nL43@P1: Puzzello, as well as the participants at the DEL brownbag series. We are grateful to the Stanford\nL44@P1: Digital Economy Lab for funding and support. All remaining errors are our own.\nL45@P1-2: 11 Introduction\nL46@P2: “Between the idea and the reality . . .\nL47@P2: falls the shadow.”\nL48@P2: T. S. Eliot, “The Hollow Men,”\nL49@P2: Monetary policy deliberations and the actual rate decision are separated by a\nL50@P2: “shadow” of persuasion and norms. Decisions by the Federal Open Market Committee\nL51@P2: (FOMC) reflect more than macroeconomic data and formal models; they also depend\nL52@P2: on members’ experience, behavioral biases, and implicit rules (e.g., Hughes and\nL53@P2: Fehrler, 2015; Haldane, 2018; Maih et al., 2021). Consequently, who participates in\nL54@P2: the deliberation and how information is communicated can influence policy outcomes\nL55@P2: beyond the underlying economic data.\nL56@P2: Precisely because of these behavioral forces, traditional models of committee\nL57@P2: decision making, though useful and mathematically elegant, often miss the complex\u0002ities of real world interactions. Behavioral economics highlights how biases, well\nL58@P2: documented by Tversky and Kahneman (1974) and shown to affect monetary policy\nL59@P2: (Haldane, 2018), can systematically shape decisions. Information design matters too:\nL60@P2: Bayesian persuasion demonstrates how agenda setters steer beliefs (Kamenica and\nL61@P2: Gentzkow, 2011). Separately, robust control preferences reveal how officials facing\nL62@P2: model uncertainty skew choices toward caution (Hansen and Sargent, 2008). Within\nL63@P2: the FOMC, policy outcomes depend on the committee’s composition, from regional\nL64@P2: voting rotation (e.g., Fos and Xu, 2025) to individual members’ policy leanings (e.g.,\nL65@P2: Hack et al., 2023).\nL66@P2: To better capture such frictions, researchers increasingly use computational\nL67@P2: approaches, such as agent-based models (Dwarakanath et al., 2024) and reinforcement\nL68@P2: learning (Hinterlang and Tänzer, 2021). Large language models (LLMs) further\nL69@P2: extend the frontier by enabling agents with coherent communication and emergent\nL70@P2: behavior. LLM-based generative agents simulate realistic social interactions (Park\nL71@P2: et al., 2022) and market dynamics (Zhang et al., 2024), opening new possibilities for\nL72@P2: policy experimentation (Argyle et al., 2022; Horton, 2023).\nL73@P2: However, no existing approach lets us observe rational and behavioral decision\nL74@P2-3: 2rules on the same information set. We address this gap with a dual track simulation\nL75@P3: framework that integrates an LLM-driven simulation of the FOMC meeting with a\nL76@P3: game theory voting model. In the rational (game theory) track, policymakers observe\nL77@P3: macro data, update beliefs via Bayesian inference, and vote to an equilibrium. In the\nL78@P3: behavioral (LLM) track, the agents get the same data, reason in natural language,\nL79@P3: engage in committee debate, and generate outcomes shaped not only by the data but\nL80@P3: also by persona heterogeneity and institutional norms that formal models struggle to\nL81@P3: capture. Recent studies show that LLM-based agents can reproduce various aspects\nL82@P3: of market dynamics (e.g., Kazinnik, 2023; Manning et al., 2024; Lopez-Lira, 2025);\nL83@P3: we apply the same logic to monetary policy. We generalize the Riboni and Ruge\u0002Murcia (2010) framework to admit heterogeneous preferences, strategic information\nL84@P3: disclosure, and a political pressure channel. Deviations between the LLM equilibrium\nL85@P3: and the analytic benchmark then isolate the behavioral frictions that standard models\nL86@P3: omit.\nL87@P3: Specifically, we construct and run two types of simulations: a set of LLM\u0002driven deliberations and a parallel Monte Carlo (MC) analysis. Both start from\nL88@P3: the same LLM-generated set of heterogeneous private opinions, reflecting FOMC\nL89@P3: members’ profiles and the current economic state. The MC simulation serves as\nL90@P3: a statistical baseline, modeling thousands of voting scenarios under theoretical\nL91@P3: framework described in Riboni and Ruge-Murcia (2010). The LLM simulation, by\nL92@P3: contrast, introduces deliberation, persuasion, and strategic interaction, capturing\nL93@P3: human-like dynamics. Comparing the two allows us to isolate and measure the\nL94@P3: impact of deliberation by identifying where LLM-driven outcomes diverge from the\nL95@P3: statistical baseline.\nL96@P3: Within the framework, we start by automatically ingesting real-time macroe\u0002conomic data, Governor speeches, district-level reports, and financial news. We\nL97@P3: then build detailed profiles for each committee member, combining their historical\nL98@P3: policy stances, biographies, recent speeches, district conditions, and the current\nL99@P3: macroeconomic context.1 Each agent draws on this information to generate an initial\nL100@P3: 1The framework supports introducing new committee members, alternative economic conditions,\nL101@P3: and other counterfactual scenarios.\nL102@P3-4: 3rate recommendation, comprising a preferred policy rate, a confidence score, and a\nL103@P4: short explanation, capturing the agent’s initial belief.\nL104@P4: With these agent profiles and initial beliefs in place, we launch the simulation\nL105@P4: to model the decision-making process under two approaches. The LLM simulation\nL106@P4: begins with several optional rounds of informal coordination to align member views.2\nL107@P4: The simulation then enters a deliberation stage, where a discussion engine records\nL108@P4: arguments and updates members’ initial beliefs, and concludes with a structured\nL109@P4: voting phase in which members submit final rate preferences for automatic aggregation\nL110@P4: and comparison. Outputs include posterior beliefs, vote patterns with dissent\nL111@P4: diagnostics, and full deliberation logs. The MC framework uses the same priors\nL112@P4: and economic parameters but removes conversational and behavioral dynamics,\nL113@P4: running thousands of rule-based voting scenarios to generate a statistical baseline.\nL114@P4: We benchmark the LLM results against MC ones.\nL115@P4: To showcase the framework, we simulate two scenarios. The first one introduces\nL116@P4: the effects of a politically charged environment on the committee. This treatment\nL117@P4: contains two distinct but related elements, designed to model both the direct pressure\nL118@P4: on the current Chair and the indirect, forward-looking behavior of the other committee\nL119@P4: members. The first component targets the Chair directly: we apply a dovish bias\nL120@P4: in the Chair’s agenda proposal. Simultaneously, the Chair’s institutional authority\nL121@P4: is diminished by removing the extra voting weight their ballot typically carries.\nL122@P4: The second component of the treatment models the strategic, forward looking\nL123@P4: behavior of the other committee members who anticipate an imminent regime change.\nL124@P4: Anticipating a more dovishly inclined successor3, members face asymmetric career\nL125@P4: incentives, so dissent costs are direction-dependent: hawkish votes carry a higher\nL126@P4: penalty, while dovish votes are cheaper, signaling alignment with the expected\nL127@P4: regime.4\nL128@P4: The second scenario examines the consequences of a sudden negative labor market\nL129@P4: data revision. This treatment feeds into the simulation as a downward adjustment\nL130@P4: 2The Monte Carlo (MC) implements the same optional mechanism.\nL131@P4: 3Needs explanation or is clear\nL132@P4: 4\nL133@P4: In the current FOMC roster, the potential candidates are Waller (90%) and Bowman (10%),\nL134@P4: reflecting speculative assessments under a dovish administration based on background, policy views,\nL135@P4: and feasibility.\nL136@P4-5: 4to the perceived strength of payroll growth, paired with an increase in the assessed\nL137@P5: uncertainty of employment sensitive members. By modeling this as a real-time\nL138@P5: information shock, we capture how the timing and nature of data updates can\nL139@P5: meaningfully reshape deliberation and decision making. In our framework, when\nL140@P5: members receive new information plays a critical role in shaping the path of discussion,\nL141@P5: belief updating, and ultimately, the policy vote.\nL142@P5: Our findings are as follows. In the baseline LLM scenario, simulating economic\nL143@P5: conditions as of late July 2025 (when the target FFR stood at 4.25%–4.50%, and\nL144@P5: effective FFR stood at 4.33%), the committee starts off with a moderately hawkish\nL145@P5: tilt, with individual rate preferences spanning 4.20% (for simulated Christopher\nL146@P5: Waller, or simWaller) to 4.62% (for simulated Alberto Musalem, or simMusalem)\nL147@P5: and clustering at the upper end of the range.5 The average initial recommendation\nL148@P5: across members is at 4.375%, with a belief spread of just over 55 basis points. As\nL149@P5: deliberations unfold, hawks emphasize the persistence of above-target inflation.6\nL150@P5: The committee converges unanimously on a final rate of 4.42%, implying an upward\nL151@P5: drift of roughly 5 basis points in the midpoint federal funds rate (4.375%) without\nL152@P5: changing the 4.25%–4.50% range. Parallel Monte Carlo simulations, which remove\nL153@P5: conversational dynamics, also converge near the existing range but yield a slightly\nL154@P5: lower mean of 4.376%. Outcomes cluster tightly and no dissents occur. Debate thus\nL155@P5: introduces a modest hawkish bias of around 5 basis points, relative to rule-based\nL156@P5: voting.\nL157@P5: Next, political pressure scenario (mirroring public attacks by the POTUS on Chair\nL158@P5: Powell) reduces the Chair’s agenda weight, applies a dovish bias to his proposal, and\nL159@P5: imposes career-driven dovish shifts for members with high appointment probabilities\nL160@P5: (simWaller, and, to lesser extent simBowman). Member views polarize: simWaller’s\nL161@P5: 5The range midpoint is 4.375%; 7 of 12 members are above it and the mean (4.39%) is slightly\nL162@P5: higher.\nL163@P5: 6For example, in one simulation, simWaller warns that ‘..if we allow inflation\nL164@P5: to spiral further, it may lead to more significant economic instability,” while sim\u0002ulated simSchmid stresses that “allowing inflation to persist unchecked can have\nL165@P5: long-term detrimental effects on both employment and economic growth,” particularly\nL166@P5: if expectations become “unanchored.” By the final discussion round, the effect of these argu\u0002ments is visible: simPowell shifts position, noting that “the current inflation data cannot\nL167@P5: be overlooked” and proposing a move to 4.50%. See Section 3.2 for background on belief formation\nL168@P5: and debate mechanics.\nL169@P5-6: 5preferred rate drops to an average of 3.97%, and he dissents in 88% of simulations.\nL170@P6: Meanwhile, simMusalem, simSchmid, and simCollins anchor their rates above 4.50%,\nL171@P6: pulling in the opposite direction. The final LLM-derived policy rate averages 4.375%.\nL172@P6: The MC benchmark under the same political pressure shows similar movement: the\nL173@P6: average final rate is 4.386%, but with nearly double the dispersion compared to the\nL174@P6: baseline. While the modal outcome remains at the upper bound (4.50%), the left tail\nL175@P6: becomes thicker: 33% of simulations end at 4.25%, and 5% drop to 4.00%. Dissent\nL176@P6: also becomes more common, occurring in 61% of meetings, with simWaller almost\nL177@P6: always the culprit. Across both tracks, political pressure nudges the committee’s\nL178@P6: center of gravity downward and erodes consensus, but without changing the standing\nL179@P6: rate range. These outcomes align with historical evidence suggesting that perceived\nL180@P6: threats to central bank independence often reshape expectations more than they\nL181@P6: alter the actual policy path (e.g., Bianchi et al., 2023).\nL182@P6: Building on this, we introduce a second treatment: a negative labor market data\nL183@P6: revision, modeled after the August 1, 2025 Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS) release.\nL184@P6: This scenario is layered on top of the political pressure context, allowing us to explore\nL185@P6: how the shocks compound.7 The data revision enters as a real-time adjustment that\nL186@P6: lowers perceived payroll strength and raises uncertainty, especially for employment\nL187@P6: sensitive members. In the LLM track, this added shock produces a further drift\nL188@P6: downward, albeit small. The average final FFR falls to 4.30%, remaining within the\nL189@P6: 4.25%–4.50% range. Dissent, again driven entirely by simWaller, rises to 73% of\nL190@P6: simulations. Relative to the baseline, this reflects a 12 basis point easing, consistent\nL191@P6: with a softer labor outlook, rather than a major policy regime shift. The MC track\nL192@P6: shows a similar downward nudge, with the mean final rate at 4.315% and a standard\nL193@P6: deviation of 15 basis points. The median remains at 4.25%, but dissent increases\nL194@P6: modestly, averaging 0.62 dissenters per meeting. Taken together, the layering of data\nL195@P6: uncertainty on top of political pressure illustrates how these shocks compound, and\nL196@P6: showcase how they can amplify disagreement, even within a constrained policy band.\nL197@P6: Our work contributes to the intersection of monetary policy committee theory,\nL198@P6: behavioral economics, and computational simulation. Building on classic committee\nL199@P6: 7Results for a stand-alone data revision shock is in the Online Appendix.\nL200@P6-7: 6models (e.g., Gerlach-Kristen, 2006; Riboni and Ruge-Murcia, 2010), we endogenize\nL201@P7: persuasion and career incentives by linking them to each member’s information set.\nL202@P7: In standard models, influence is imposed exogenously by a chair or agenda setter;\nL203@P7: here, influence attempts and their costs arise organically during debate. Unlike\nL204@P7: Bayesian persuasion models (e.g., Kamenica and Gentzkow, 2011), which feature\nL205@P7: one-to-many communication from a single informed sender, our environment allows\nL206@P7: many-to-many strategic exchanges, reflecting the interactive flow of arguments in\nL207@P7: actual FOMC meetings. Embedding reputation driven career concerns (Holmström,\nL208@P7: 1999) captures how members may adjust their positions to align with current or\nL209@P7: anticipated leaders. This creates a novel channel for political pressure on the Chair\nL210@P7: to spread: members reposition in anticipation of a leadership change, shifting the\nL211@P7: committee’s stance before the change occurs. In sum, our model endogenizes both\nL212@P7: persuasion and career incentives, extending committee theory toward a more realistic\nL213@P7: representation of decision making.\nL214@P7: Second, we link the rational choice committee model to behavioral research\nL215@P7: on cognitive biases, transparency, and communication in policy decisions. Prior\nL216@P7: work shows that policymakers’ personal experiences can systematically shape their\nL217@P7: hawkish or dovish leanings: FOMC members who have lived through high inflation\nL218@P7: forecast higher inflation and favor tighter policy, whereas those with benign inflation\nL219@P7: histories lean dovish (Malmendier and Nagel, 2016). We embed this experience-based\nL220@P7: heterogeneity into our agent profiles, recognizing that members carry distinct biases\nL221@P7: and personal histories into deliberations. By integrating these behavioral factors into\nL222@P7: a formal voting model, we examine how cognitive biases and institutional features\nL223@P7: interact to shape policy outcomes.\nL224@P7: Third, our work bridges two rapidly growing strands of computational economics:\nL225@P7: agent-based modeling with reinforcement learning for policy design (e.g., Peters\nL226@P7: et al., 2022; Cook and Palmer, 2025), and the use of large language model (LLM)\nL227@P7: agents to replicate realistic human interaction patterns (e.g., Manning et al., 2024).\nL228@P7: Recent studies have begun applying these methods to central banking; for instance,\nL229@P7: Seok et al. (2025) simulate deliberative exchanges among LLM agents in stylized\nL230@P7: monetary policy settings. Our framework differs in scope and design: we create\nL231@P7-8: 7a dual-track environment where deliberative and rational voting behaviors run in\nL232@P8: parallel, grounded in real-time macroeconomic data and detailed FOMC member\nL233@P8: profiles.\nL234@P8: More broadly, this simulation framework provides a controlled environment to\nL235@P8: stress test various scenarios. We can experiment in silico (Horton, 2023) with factors\nL236@P8: like disclosure rules, dissent penalties, or coalition dynamics to see how they affect\nL237@P8: decision making under various scenarios. By enabling counterfactual experimentation\nL238@P8: in a realistic setting, this framework lets policymakers and researchers explore various\nL239@P8: “what if ” scenarios and observe the possible impacts on committee behavior and\nL240@P8: policy.\nL241@P8: The remainder of the paper proceeds as follows. We review the FOMC meeting\nL242@P8: process in Section 2. We then outline our framework in Section 3, including the data\nL243@P8: collection, construction of agent personas, the multi-stage simulation design, as well\nL244@P8: as the voting process; Section 3.2 describes the game theory implementation and\nL245@P8: Section 3.3 describes the Monte Carlo simulation framework. Section 4 explores our\nL246@P8: counterfactual scenario and describes the results. Section 5 concludes with some\nL247@P8: policy implications.\nL248@P8: 2 Modeling FOMC Meetings: Background\nL249@P8: “The debate on monetary policy is going on 365 days a year for 24 hours a day, all\nL250@P8: around the world. The meetings are definitely important, but they’re a snapshot of the\nL251@P8: ongoing debate.”\nL252@P8: — Former FOMC member James Bullard, in an interview8\nL253@P8: FOMC meetings are highly structured deliberations that anchor U.S. monetary\nL254@P8: policy. However, as James Bullard, former president of the Federal Reserve Bank of\nL255@P8: St. Louis, suggests, they represent only a moment in time within a broader, ongoing\nL256@P8: policy debate. While the meetings formalize key decisions, the underlying policy\nL257@P8: dialogue spans months, both within and beyond the Fed.\nL258@P8: Each meeting typically follows a fixed agenda: staff economists present updated\nL259@P8: 8See Hyatt (2024) for the full interview.\nL260@P8-9: 8forecasts and alternative scenarios; FOMC members then offer prepared statements\nL261@P9: outlining their interpretations of the data and views on appropriate policy.9 Following\nL262@P9: this stage, the Chair opens the floor for discussion, where members respond to others’\nL263@P9: arguments, and debate policy options. Finally, the committee votes on the policy\nL264@P9: decision. Dissenting votes are relatively rare and, when they occur, serve mainly\nL265@P9: to register an alternative viewpoint rather than to overturn the majority consensus\nL266@P9: (Thornton and Wheelock, 2014).\nL267@P9: Our simulation mirrors the FOMC’s workflow. The sequence of events, the\nL268@P9: information available to each participant, and the conversational norms governing\nL269@P9: discussion are modeled to reflect actual FOMC dynamics. Because monetary policy\nL270@P9: is an ongoing debate rather than a static decision, our framework can continuously\nL271@P9: ingest “fresh” economic and market data, dynamically updating the simulation to\nL272@P9: reflect evolving conditions. Each FOMC participant in our framework is represented\nL273@P9: by an independent agent (implemented via an LLM) with a distinct profile. In\nL274@P9: the baseline setup, these agents correspond to the twelve voting members of that\nL275@P9: year’s FOMC. However, the framework can easily extensible to include non-voting\nL276@P9: participants, other attendees, or hypothetical members. We describe the simulation\nL277@P9: pipeline next.\nL278@P9: 3 LLM Simulation Framework\nL279@P9: Standard models of committee voting assume members hold rational expectations,\nL280@P9: update beliefs via Bayes’ rule, and vote to minimize well-defined loss functions.\nL281@P9: These assumptions yield testable predictions about voting patterns, dissent, and\nL282@P9: policy outcomes. Empirically, however, FOMC behavior diverges sharply from those\nL283@P9: forecasts. Between 1978 and 2023 fewer than 6% of all recorded ballots were dissents\nL284@P9: (Thornton and Wheelock, 2014), and from 2005 to 2022 not a single Board Governor\nL285@P9: dissented (Bobrov et al., 2025). Archival transcripts and insider accounts show\nL286@P9: that members undergo extensive staff briefings and private discussions in the week\nL287@P9: before each meeting, typically converging on the Chair’s proposal well in advance\nL288@P9: 9Both the Tealbook reports and participants’ prepared statements are made public with a\nL289@P9: five-year lag; the Philadelphia Fed hosts the historical files.\nL290@P9-10: 9of the public vote (Bernanke, 2022). Dissent costs are also role-specific: Reserve\nL291@P10: Bank presidents, who face weaker career constraints, cast the vast majority of\nL292@P10: dissents, whereas D.C.-based Governors almost always vote with the Chair (Chappell\nL293@P10: and McGregor, 2013). Added to this are external pressures: political jaw-boning\nL294@P10: measurably impacts interest rate futures (Bianchi et al., 2023), while heightened\nL295@P10: transparency can induce conformity (Hughes and Fehrler, 2015). In short, pre\u0002meeting coordination, consensus norms, career incentives, and political or market\nL296@P10: pressures all interact to influence the real-world outcomes away from the predictions\nL297@P10: of textbook voting models. Capturing these informal frictions is therefore central to\nL298@P10: any realistic simulation of FOMC decision-making.\nL299@P10: LLMs enhance economic modeling in this context by integrating data, institutional\nL300@P10: rules, personal incentives, and strategic context in ways that mirror how Federal\nL301@P10: Reserve officials reason. And because LLMs converse naturally, they can explicitly\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2209.06899] Out of One, Many: Using Language Models to Simulate Human Samples (https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.06899)\nciteturn120view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.06899\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 170\n",
research/source_ledgers/literature/current_oct09/web_retrieval_records.json:4:  "followup": "The threat of analytic flexibility in using large language models to simulate human data: A call to attention (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13397)\nciteturn121academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Title: The threat of analytic flexibility in using large language models to simulate human data: A call to attentionAuthors: Jamie Cummins ... Social scientists are now using large language models to create \"silicon samples\" - synthetic datasets intended to stand in for human respondents, aimed at revolutionising human subjects research.\nTitle: The threat of analytic flexibility in using large language models to simulate human data: A call to attention\nAuthors: Jamie Cummins\nDate: Tue Sep 16 17:29:47 2025\n\nSocial scientists are now using large language models to create \"silicon samples\" - synthetic datasets intended to stand in for human respondents, aimed at revolutionising human subjects research. However, there are many analytic choices which must be made to produce these samples. Though many of these choices are defensible, their impact on sample quality is poorly understood. I map out these analytic choices and demonstrate how a very small number of decisions can dramatically change the correspondence between silicon samples and human data. Configurations (N = 252) varied substantially in their capacity to estimate (i) rank ordering of participants, (ii) response distributions, and (iii) between-scale correlations. Most critically, configurations were not consistent in quality: those that performed well on one dimension often performed poorly on another, implying that there is no \"one-size-fits-all\" configuration that optimises the accuracy of these samples. I call for greater attention to the threat of analytic flexibility in using silicon samples.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPolitical Analysis (2024), 32, 401–416 (https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/B92267DC26195C7F36E63EA04A47D2FE/S1047198724000056a.pdf/synthetic-replacements-for-human-survey-data-the-perils-of-large-language-models.pdf)\nciteturn121search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; # Synthetic Replacements for Human Survey Data? ... Abstract box with heading “Abstract” and a paragraph stating that large language models (LLMs) offer new research possibilities for social scientists but their potential as “synthetic data” is still largely unknown; the paper investigates how accurately ChatGPT can recover public opinion by adopting different “personas” and providing feeling thermometer scores for 11 sociopolitical groups. ... Given the rising expense and difficulty of interviewing respondents, researchers increasingly turn to other methods of characterizing public opinion and sentiment, such as relying on non-survey data (Beauchamp 2017; Tucker 2017) or implementing sophisticated weighting methods (Bisbee 2019; Caughey and Warshaw 2015; Gelman 1997; Ghitza and Gelman 2013; Goplerud 2024; Lax and Phillips 2009). ... Published by Cambridge University Press on behalf of The Society for Political Methodology.\nPolitical Analysis (2024), 32, 401–416  \ndoi:10.1017/pan.2024.5\n\nARTICLE\n\n# Synthetic Replacements for Human Survey Data?  \n## The Perils of Large Language Models\n\nJames Bisbee, Joshua D. Clinton, Cassy Dorff, Brenton Kenkel and Jennifer M. Larson\n\nPolitical Science Department, Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN, USA\n\n**Corresponding author:** James Bisbee; Email: james.h.bisbee@vanderbilt.edu\n\n(Received 2 May 2023; revised 18 January 2024; accepted 20 January 2024; published online 17 May 2024)\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nAbstract box with heading “Abstract” and a paragraph stating that large language models (LLMs) offer new research possibilities for social scientists but their potential as “synthetic data” is still largely unknown; the paper investigates how accurately ChatGPT can recover public opinion by adopting different “personas” and providing feeling thermometer scores for 11 sociopolitical groups. It reports that ChatGPT averages correspond closely to the 2016–2020 ANES baseline survey, but sampling by ChatGPT is not reliable for statistical inference because there is less variation than in real surveys and regression coefficients often differ significantly from ANES-based estimates. It also documents how synthetic response distributions vary with minor prompt wording changes and how the same prompt yields significantly different results over a 3-month period. The conclusion is that these findings raise serious concerns about the quality, reliability, and reproducibility of synthetic survey data generated by LLMs.\n</visual_element>\n\n**Keywords:** ChatGPT; synthetic data; public opinion; research ethics\n\n**Edited by:** Jeff Gill\n\nPublic opinion polling is seemingly in a crisis (Shapiro 2019). Costs are increasing, response rates are declining (Keeter *et al.* 2017), and there are growing concerns about inaccuracy (Clinton *et al.* 2021a; Clinton, Lapinski, and Trussler 2022; Kennedy *et al.* 2018). At the same time, polls are necessary tools to assess and address growing concerns about polarization and democratic backsliding (Graham 2023; Waldner and Lust 2018). When done well, public opinion polls allow scholars, policymakers, and journalists to assess the opinions of the whole public—not just those who are the most active, willing, and able to express their opinions through more costly means like direct appeals, protests, and donations.\n\nGiven the rising expense and difficulty of interviewing respondents, researchers increasingly turn to other methods of characterizing public opinion and sentiment, such as relying on non-survey data (Beauchamp 2017; Tucker 2017) or implementing sophisticated weighting methods (Bisbee 2019; Caughey and Warshaw 2015; Gelman 1997; Ghitza and Gelman 2013; Goplerud 2024; Lax and Phillips 2009).\n\nLarge language models (LLMs) that synthesize vast corpora of human-generated text might look like the new frontier in characterizing public opinion without the expense of traditional polling. Social scientists have already used LLMs to label political data (Gilardi, Alizadeh, and Kubli 2023; Törnberg 2023), estimate politicians’ ideology (Wu *et al.* 2023), and generate synthetic samples for pilot testing (Argyle *et al.* 2023; Horton 2023). At least one start-up in private industry suggests that\n\n© The Author(s), 2024. Published by Cambridge University Press on behalf of The Society for Political Methodology.  \nThis is an Open Access article, distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution licence (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0), which permits unrestricted re-use, distribution and reproduction, provided the original article is properly cited.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSynthetic Replacements for Human Survey Data? The Perils of Large Language Models (https://ideas.repec.org/a/cup/polals/v32y2024i4p401-416_2.html)\nciteturn121search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Crawled: today; # Synthetic Replacements for Human Survey Data? ...   * Bisbee, James ... Large language models (LLMs) offer new research possibilities for social scientists, but their potential as “synthetic data” is still largely unknown. ... The Perils of Large Language Models,\" Political Analysis, Cambridge University Press, vol. 32(4), pages 401-416, October.\n\n# Synthetic Replacements for Human Survey Data? The Perils of Large Language Models\n\n## Author\n\nListed:\n\n  * Bisbee, James\n  * Clinton, Joshua D.\n  * Dorff, Cassy\n  * Kenkel, Brenton\n  * Larson, Jennifer M.\n\nRegistered:\n\n## Abstract\n\nLarge language models (LLMs) offer new research possibilities for social scientists, but their potential as “synthetic data” is still largely unknown. In this paper, we investigate how accurately the popular LLM ChatGPT can recover public opinion, prompting the LLM to adopt different “personas” and then provide feeling thermometer scores for 11 sociopolitical groups. The average scores generated by ChatGPT correspond closely to the averages in our baseline survey, the 2016–2020 American National Election Study (ANES). Nevertheless, sampling by ChatGPT is not reliable for statistical inference: there is less variation in responses than in the real surveys, and regression coefficients often differ significantly from equivalent estimates obtained using ANES data. We also document how the distribution of synthetic responses varies with minor changes in prompt wording, and we show how the same prompt yields significantly different results over a 3-month period. Altogether, our findings raise serious concerns about the quality, reliability, and reproducibility of synthetic survey data generated by LLMs.\n\n## Suggested Citation\n\n* Bisbee, James & Clinton, Joshua D. & Dorff, Cassy & Kenkel, Brenton & Larson, Jennifer M., 2024. \"Synthetic Replacements for Human Survey Data? The Perils of Large Language Models,\" Political Analysis, Cambridge University Press, vol. 32(4), pages 401-416, October.\nHandle: RePEc:cup:polals:v:32:y:2024:i:4:p:401-416_2--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe threat of analytic flexibility in using large language models to simulate human data: A call to attention (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/395583106_The_threat_of_analytic_flexibility_in_using_large_language_models_to_simulate_human_data_A_call_to_attention/fulltext/68cbb533a8689b51bd6093b0/The-threat-of-analytic-flexibility-in-using-large-language-models-to-simulate-human-data-A-call-to-attention.pdf)\nciteturn121search14 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; The threat of analytic flexibility in using large language models to simulate human data: A call to attentionJamie Cummins ... The capacity of these models to produce sensical natural language output to queries, as well as their flexibility, efficiency, and generalisability, has led researchers to explore their use in tasks across the research process. ... For example, Hewitt et al. (2025) evaluated whether GPT-4 could simulate the results of social science experiments by creating a silicon sample of individual participants with specific demographic profiles.\nThe threat of analytic flexibility in using large language models to simulate human data: A call to attention\n\nJamie Cummins  \nUniversity of Bern, Switzerland  \nUniversity of Oxford, United Kingdom\n\nSocial scientists are now using large language models to create “silicon samples” - synthetic datasets intended to stand in for human respondents, aimed at revolutionising human subjects research. However, there are many analytic choices which must be made to produce these samples. Though many of these choices are defensible, their impact on sample quality is poorly understood. I map out these analytic choices and demonstrate how a very small number of decisions can dramatically change the correspondence between silicon samples and human data. Configurations (N = 252) varied substantially in their capacity to estimate (i) rank ordering of participants, (ii) response distributions, and (iii) between-scale correlations. Most critically, configurations were not consistent in quality: those that performed well on one dimension often performed poorly on another, implying that there is no “one-size-fits-all” configuration that optimises the accuracy of these samples. I call for greater attention to the threat of analytic flexibility in using silicon samples.\n\nGenerative large language models (LLMs) have ushered in a sea change within the social sciences. The capacity of these models to produce sensical natural language output to queries, as well as their flexibility, efficiency, and generalisability, has led researchers to explore their use in tasks across the research process. One of the most ambitious proposed uses of LLMs in this respect has been to use them as simulated research participants to determine how human participants might respond to a particular context, set of survey questions, or intervention (Cui et al., 2025). This is based on the tacit assumption that, on some level, the corpuses of data which LLMs are trained on may enable them to approximate similar data-generating processes to those which underpin human responses (Z. Lin, 2025b). If achievable, this could have a transformative impact on the social sciences. It would enable researchers to predict the responses of vulnerable and/or hard-to-reach populations of participants without the need for time- and resource-intensive research studies, improving the representativeness and diversity of research (Sarstedt et al., 2024). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe threat of analytic flexibility in using large language models to simulate human data | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2509.13397)\nciteturn121search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 6 days ago; ## The Concept of Analytic Flexibility ... The research by Cummins explores how these seemingly minor decisions can lead to vastly different scientific conclusions. ... @misc{cummins2026threatanalyticflexibilityusing, title={The threat of analytic flexibility in using large language models to simulate human data}, author={Jamie Cummins}, year={2026}, eprint={2509.13397}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.CY}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13397}, }\n## BibTeX\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{cummins2026threatanalyticflexibilityusing, title={The threat of analytic flexibility in using large language models to simulate human data}, author={Jamie Cummins}, year={2026}, eprint={2509.13397}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.CY}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13397}, }------------