 large language models.* (See page: 2) (https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.754.pdf)\nciteturn121search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; James Bisbee, Joshua Clinton, Cassy Dorff, Brenton Kenkel, and Jennifer Larson. ... *Synthetic replacements for human survey data? ... * *Science*, 356(6334):183–186. ... *Raft: Reward ranked finetuning for generative foundation model alignment.\nJames Bisbee, Joshua Clinton, Cassy Dorff, Brenton Kenkel, and Jennifer Larson. 2023. *Synthetic replacements for human survey data? the perils of large language models.* (See page: 2)\n\nSu Lin Blodgett, Solon Barocas, Hal Daumé III, and Hanna Wallach. 2020. *Language (technology) is power: A critical survey of “bias” in NLP.* In *Proceedings of the 58th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics*, pages 5454–5476, Online. Association for Computational Linguistics. (See page: 2)\n\nSu Lin Blodgett, Gilsinia Lopez, Alexandra Olteanu, Robert Sim, and Hanna Wallach. 2021. *Stereotyping Norwegian salmon: An inventory of pitfalls in fairness benchmark datasets.* In *Proceedings of the 59th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics and the 11th International Joint Conference on Natural Language Processing (Volume 1: Long Papers)*, pages 1004–1015, Online. Association for Computational Linguistics. (See pages: 3, 6, and 14)\n\nRalph Allan Bradley and Milton E. Terry. 1952. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhile Chatbots Have Many Answers, Do They Have Good Questions? An Experimental Study Exploring the Creation and Evaluation of Survey Questions Using Automated Chatbot Tools (https://surveydatascience.isr.umich.edu/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/Trent-Buskirk-SLIDES-Likert-2025.pdf)\nciteturn121search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; Bisbee and colleagues (2023) report contrary findings that suggest that silicon samples generate responses that are far less variable compared to actual survey respondents’ responses. ... Screenshot of a Political Analysis journal article page titled “Synthetic Replacements for Human Survey Data?\nLLMs for Survey Responses: Silicon or Synthetic Samples\n\nBisbee and colleagues (2023) report contrary findings that suggest that silicon samples generate responses that are far less variable compared to actual survey respondents’ responses. They also remark that results can be highly dependent on prompt and LLM version being used.\n\nhttps://bit.ly/BisbeeEtAl2024\n\nSun and colleagues (2023): Improved upon the concept of silicon samples by introducing so called “random silicon sampling” and showed it performed as well or better than silicon sampling for many tasks.\n\nhttps://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.18144\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nTop-right icons: a blue-outlined square with three stylized people and check/cross speech bubbles, next to the green OpenAI logo.\n</visual_element>\n\n<visual_element id=\"e2\">\nScreenshot of a Political Analysis journal article page titled “Synthetic Replacements for Human Survey Data? The Perils of Large Language Models,” showing publication date 17 May 2024, author names, tabs (Article, Figures, Supplementary materials, Metrics), buttons (Save PDF, Share, Cite, Rights & Permissions), and the start of the abstract.\n</visual_element>\n\n<visual_element id=\"e3\">\nScreenshot of an academic paper title page headed “Random Silicon Sampling: Simulating Human Sub-Population Opinion Using a Large Language Model Based on Group-Level Demographic Information,” with author list and the beginning of the abstract in two-column layout.\n</visual_element>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Future (https://isr.umich.edu/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Kreuter-The-Future-of-Data-Collection.pdf)\nciteturn121search18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.9 years ago; Bisbee, J., Clinton, J., Dorff, C., Kenkel, B., & Larson, J. ... Synthetic Replacements for Human Survey Data?\nFigure 2: Average feeling \nthermometer results (x-axis) \nfor different target groups (y- \naxes) by party ID of \nrespondent (columns).\nAverage ANES estimates \nfrom the 2016 and 2020 \nwaves indicated with red \ntriangles and one standard \ndeviation indicated with thick \nred bars. LLM-derived \naverages indicated by black \ncircles and thin black bars.\nSample sizes for each \ngroup-wise comparison are \nidentical.\nBisbee, J., Clinton, J., Dorff, C., Kenkel, B., & Larson, J. (2023, May 4). \nSynthetic Replacements for Human Survey Data? The Perils of Large \nLanguage Models. https://doi.org/10.31235/osf.io/5ecfa\n22--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPredicting Real-World Quantitative Data with Synthetic Populations — Synthetic Outcomes (https://outcomes.syntheticusers.com/posts/predicting-quantitative-data-with-synthetic-populations/)\nciteturn121search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: last week; In a large preregistered evaluation across 70 survey experiments, treatment effects estimated from LLM-simulated samples correlated with the real effects about as strongly as pooled human forecasters did — and the correlation held up for studies published after the model’s training cutoff (Ashokkumar et al., 2026). ... Separate audits find synthetic respondents breaking down on exactly the subgroup structure that most inference depends on (Bisbee et al., 2024). ... Bisbee, J., Clinton, J. ... Synthetic Replacements for Human Survey Data? ... Danish Institute for Educational Research.\n\nBisbee, J., Clinton, J. D., Dorff, C., Kenkel, B., & Larson, J. M. (2024). Synthetic Replacements for Human Survey Data? The Perils of Large Language Models. Political Analysis, 32(4), 401–416.\n\nCronbach, L. J., Gleser, G. C., Nanda, H., & Rajaratnam, N. (1972). The Dependability of Behavioral Measurements: Theory of Generalizability for Scores and Profiles. Wiley.\n\nde Finetti, B. (1937). La prévision: ses lois logiques, ses sources subjectives. Annales de l’Institut Henri Poincaré, 7(1), 1–68.\n\nDeming, W. E., & Stephan, F. F. (1940). On a Least Squares Adjustment of a Sampled Frequency Table When the Expected Marginal Totals Are Known. The Annals of Mathematical Statistics, 11(4), 427–444.\n\nGelman, A., & Little, T. C. (1997). Poststratification into Many Categories Using Hierarchical Logistic Regression. Survey Methodology, 23(2), 127–135.\n\nGretton, A., Borgwardt, K. M., Rasch, M. J., Schölkopf, B., & Smola, A. (2012). A Kernel Two-Sample Test. Journal of Machine Learning Research, 13, 723–773.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFOMC <i>In Silico</i>: A Multi-Agent System for Monetary Policy Decision Modeling by Sophia Kazinnik, Tara M. Sinclair :: SSRN (https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=5424097)\nciteturn121search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; George Washington University - Department of Economics; George Washington University - Elliott School of International Affairs (ESIA); George Washington University - Institute For International Economic Policy (GWIIEP); George Washington University - Research Program on Forecasting; George Washington University - George Washington Institute of Public Policy (GWIPP); Australian National University (ANU) - Centre for Applied Macroeconomic Analysis (CAMA); Halle Institute for Economic Research ... In a backtest of 218 FOMC meetings from 2000 through June 2026, the framework predicts the policy rate with a mean absolute error of 8.3 basis points, comes within 25 basis points in 94 percent of meetings, and correctly classifies hikes, cuts, and holds in 91 percent. ... Kazinnik, Sophia and Sinclair, Tara M., FOMC In Silico: A Multi-Agent System for Monetary Policy Decision Modeling (August 31, 2025).\nIn a backtest of 218 FOMC meetings from 2000 through June 2026, the framework predicts the policy rate with a mean absolute error of 8.3 basis points, comes within 25 basis points in 94 percent of meetings, and correctly classifies hikes, cuts, and holds in 91 percent. Accuracy is similar before and after the model’s training cutoff, suggesting the results are not driven by memorization. We then use the validated framework for counterfactual experiments. Committee rules can block a politically captured chair, but not public persuasion; committee decisions differ  from those produced by mechanical decision rules; composition matters far more than the chair or formal mandate; and deliberation builds consensus rather than improving forecasts. The framework offers a new laboratory for studying monetary policymaking and institutional design.\n\nKeywords: Generative AI, Multi-Agent Systems, Large Language Models, Federal Open Market Committee, Monetary Policy, Simulations\n\nLicense Information\n\nThe copyright holder has granted SSRN a license. All rights reserved. No reuse allowed without permission.\n\nJEL Classification: E52, E58, C63, D83, C73\n\nSuggested Citation\n\nKazinnik, Sophia and Sinclair, Tara M., FOMC In Silico: A Multi-Agent System for Monetary Policy Decision Modeling (August 31, 2025). Available at SSRN: https://ssrn.com/abstract=5424097 or http://dx.doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.5424097\n\n### Sophia Kazinnik (Contact Author)\n\n#### Stanford University ( email )\n\n367 Panama St\nStanford, CA 94305\nUnited States\n\n### Tara M. Sinclair\n\n#### George Washington University - Department of Economics ( email )\n\n2115 G Street NW\nMonroe Hall Suite 340\nWashington, DC 20052\nUnited States\n202-944-7988 (Phone)\n202-994-6147 (Fax)\n\n\nDownload This Paper\n\nOpen PDF in Browser\n\n### 43 References\n\n## Paper statistics\n\nDownloads\n\n987\n\nAbstract Views\n\n2,885\n\n43 References--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhen Can LLM Digital Twins Reduce Human Measurement? From Behavioral Fidelity to Statistical Substitutability | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2609.07987)\nciteturn121search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last week; This has encouraged researchers to build LLM digital twins: model-based representations of particular people created from demographic information, psychological traits, prior survey responses, or free-text biographies. ... For confirmatory research, investigators should define the estimand first, reserve an independent human validation sample, measure residualized paired signal, estimate feasible finite-sample variance reduction, and test confidence-interval coverage and population stability. ... Synthetic Replacements for Human Survey Data? ... This study directly supports the paper’s central warning that synthetic respondents may reproduce averages while misrepresenting variation, subgroup patterns, and conditional relationships. ... Bisbee, J.\n\nDatabase report: Twin-2k-500: A data set for building digital twins of over 2,000 people based on their answers to over 500 questions\n\nThe Twin-2K-500 dataset is the paper’s primary empirical benchmark, supplying information-rich respondent profiles, human outcomes, and released digital-twin predictions across behavioral tasks. The present study reanalyzes this resource to test whether predictive performance translates into statistical substitutability.\n\nOlivier Toubia, George Z Gui, Tianyi Peng, Daniel J Merlau, Ang Li, and Haozhe Chen. Database report: Twin-2k-500: A data set for building digital twins of over 2,000 people based on their answers to over 500 questions. Marketing Science, 44(6):1446–1455, 2025.\n\nLLM Agents Grounded in Self-Reports Enable General-Purpose Simulation of Individuals\n\nThis work is a key antecedent for information-rich, person-specific digital twins and motivates the expectation that extensive self-reports can improve held-out individual prediction. The paper uses it to distinguish behavioral prediction accuracy from the stronger requirement of inferential usefulness.\n\nJ. S. Park, C. Q. Zou, J. Kamphorst, N. Egan, A. Shaw, B. M. Hill, C. Cai, M. R. Morris, P. Liang, R. Willer, and M. S. Bernstein. LLM Agents Grounded in Self-Reports Enable General-Purpose Simulation of Individuals. arXiv preprint arXiv:2411.10109, 2024.\n\nSynthetic Replacements for Human Survey Data? The Perils of Large Language Models\n\nThis study directly supports the paper’s central warning that synthetic respondents may reproduce averages while misrepresenting variation, subgroup patterns, and conditional relationships. It provides important evidence motivating the distinction between aggregate behavioral fidelity and respondent-level statistical substitutability.\n\nJ. Bisbee, J. D. Clinton, C. Dorff, B. Kenkel, and J. M. Larson. Synthetic Replacements for Human Survey Data? The Perils of Large Language Models. Political Analysis, 32(4):401–416, 2024.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombining Synthetic and Human Respondents: The Math (https://personahive.ai/blog/combining-synthetic-and-human-respondents)\nciteturn121search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Bisbee and colleagues examined language model responses as substitutes for human survey data and reported divergences that are unstable across model versions. ... **Does this mean synthetic research needs human fieldwork to be useful? ...   * Synthetic Replacements for Human Survey Data? ... Gubler, Christopher Rytting, David Wingate, Political Analysis (2023), Cambridge University Press\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2607.26348] When Synthetic Users Fail: A Cross-Domain Benchmark of LLM-Simulated Human Survey Responses - Academus scientific article reader (https://academ.us/article/2607.26348/)\nciteturn121search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; A single protocol, run across four models spanning two families and an 8B-to-frontier capability range, is applied to two independent domains of real human-response data: U.S.general social attitudes (General Social Survey) and cross-cultural values (World Values Survey).Every model is benchmarked against a suite of non-LLM baselines fit on held-out human data. ... Running a human study is slow and expensive, so a fast-growing body of work proposes replacing human participants with large language models (LLMs) prompted to behave as synthetic users, using them to pilot surveys, pre-test questionnaire items, estimate opinion distributions, and stand in for study participants. ... The World Values Survey (WVS) Wave 7, 63 countries, 16 ordinal value questions spanning economic values, corruption perceptions, immigration attitudes, views of science, religiosity, and moral justifiability.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nYour Polls On ChatGPT (https://www.verasight.io/reports/synthetic-sampling)\nciteturn121search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; A popular method is to give a large language model (LLM) a set of demographic and political traits belonging to a real, human person, and ask the model how that person would respond on a set of survey questions. ... First, we will use a LLM to generate a set of fake responses to two poll questions — a \"synthetic sample” — using the demographic and political variables for 1,500 people from our real survey. ... Following several existing studies of LLM-generated polling data  (Bisbee et al. ... One note on LLMs: different providers offer different models — trained in different ways and containing different systems for “thinking” or external research — for users to interface with.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBias Diagnostics for LLM-Powered Survey Instruments in Economic Polling — clawRxiv (https://clawrxiv.io/abs/2604.02025)\nciteturn121search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 mon